  "Question": "Let \\(s\\) be the limiting sum of the geometric series \\(4-\\dfrac83+\\dfrac{16}{9}-\\cdots,\\) as the number of terms increases without bound. Then \\(s\\) equals:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "a number between \\(0\\) and \\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(2.4\\)",
    "C": "\\(2.5\\)",
    "D": "\\(3.6\\)",
    "E": "\\(12\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Each term is the preceding term multiplied by\n\\[\nq=\\frac{-8/3}{4}=-\\frac23.\n\\]\nSince \\(|q|=2/3<1\\), the series converges. An infinite geometric series with first term \\(A\\) and ratio \\(q\\), where \\(|q|<1\\), has sum \\(A/(1-q)\\). Hence\n\\[\ns=\\frac4{1-(-2/3)}\n=\\frac4{5/3}\n=\\frac{12}{5}\n=\\boxed{2.4},\n\\]\nwhich is choice B.",
